,p_depending_on_condition_type=>'EQUALS'
,p_depending_on_expression=>'Y'
,p_examples=>wwv_flow_string.join(wwv_flow_t_varchar2(
'<dl>',
'<dt>Example with Start Date only (for non-duration based events)</dt>',
'<dd><pre>',
'begin',
'    update emp',
'       set hiredate = to_date(:APEX$NEW_START_DATE, ''YYYYMMDDHH24MISS'')',
'     where empno = :APEX$PK_VALUE;',
'end;',
'</pre></dd>',
'<dt>Example with Start and End dates. (for duration based events)</dt>',
'<dd><pre>',
'begin',
'    update projects',
'       set start_date = to_date(:APEX$NEW_START_DATE, ''YYYYMMDDHH24MISS''),',
'           end_date = to_date(:APEX$NEW_END_DATE, ''YYYYMMDDHH24MISS'')',
'     where id = :APEX$PK_VALUE;',
'end;',
'</pre></dd>',
'</dl>'))
,p_help_text=>wwv_flow_string.join(wwv_flow_t_varchar2(
'<p>Enter the PL/SQL code to update the underlying tables or updateable views when a calendar entry is updated using drag and drop.</p>',
'<p>If this calendar shows duration-based events (by selecting a column for End Date), then the PL/SQL code also needs to update this value. If the whole event is dragged, then the End Date maintains the current duration. If the end of the event is dr'
||'agged, the duration of the event is updated. In such cases the start date will be unchanged, but the end date is updated to reflect the revised duration.</p>',
'<p>The calendar substitution variables include:</p>',
'<dl>',
'  <dt>APEX$NEW_START_DATE</dt>',
'    <dd>Returns the revised start date. The date format is <code>''YYYYMMDDHH24MISS''</code> even if the <em>Show Time</em> for this calendar is <strong>No</strong>.</dd>',
'  <dt>APEX$NEW_END_DATE</dt>',
'    <dd>Returns the revised end date for duration based events. The date format is <code>''YYYYMMDDHH24MISS''</code> even if the <em>Show Time</em> for this calendar is <strong>No</strong>.</dd>',
'  <dt>APEX$PK_VALUE</dt>',
'    <dd>Returns the value of the column specified in the Primary Key Column.</dd>',
'</dl>'))
);
