,p_attribute_type=>'TEXT'
,p_is_required=>false
,p_is_common=>false
,p_display_length=>30
,p_max_length=>128
,p_is_translatable=>false
,p_help_text=>wwv_flow_string.join(wwv_flow_t_varchar2(
'<p>Select the column that identifies the version of the row, this column does not need to be present on the page. Leave this attribute blank to use the built-in checksum.</p>',
'<p>Opportunistic locking uses lost update detection to ensure data integrity in applications where data can be accessed concurrently. &PRODUCT_NAME. can use a built in checksum to perform lost update detection and determine the version of the row. Th'
||'e checksum is a hash function of all the column values for the specific table row. It is retrieved when the record is queried from the database in <em>Automatic Row Fetch</em>, and then compared against the hash of the current record in the database '
||'as part of this process. If the two values are the same then the update proceeds, otherwise an error is raised by this process.</p>',
'<p>This attribute enables you to specify a single column which holds the row version, as an alternative to using the built-in checksum processing. This column should be updated whenever a record in the database is updated. Oracle recommends implement'
||'ing a <em>Before Update</en> database trigger on the table to increment or otherwise update the row version column. NUMBER columns are presumed to contain a sequence number for the row. However, DATE and TIMESTAMP columns can also be used. The advant'
||'age of using a single column is that the &PRODUCT_NAME. engine does not need to perform the hash of the complete row, instead it can just compare a single column value.</p>'))
);
